Cursor's plan (train on 2 volumes, validate on the 3rd) deviates from Section III-I's actual established protocol: each volume (A, B, C) is split by z-slice — first 70 sections train, middle 30 validation, last 25 test, per volume, not whole-volume holdout. Whole-volume holdout would mean your reported numbers no longer match the split [26]/[18] used, which breaks the exact comparability your whole evaluation depends on. This needs fixing in the data-loading step before any full run.

Correction 2: CREMI's instance/semantic target should be clefts (synapses), not neuron_ids

This is the deeper issue. neuron_ids has no background (every voxel belongs to some neuron) — that's not a bug, it's just the wrong label for your task. Your baselines ([26] ReIsoSeg specifically) evaluate CREMI on synapse/cleft segmentation, not generic neuron segmentation. Using neuron_ids tests a completely different task than what your comparison claims are about. The correct target is clefts — which does have real background (most voxels aren't synapses) and matches what [26] actually reports numbers on.

One more detail: clefts contains the sentinel value 18446744073709551615 for "unlabeled" — this must be mapped to background (0), not treated as a real instance, before generating BCD labels.

